<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Mzab confederation</h1><p class="st-meta">1012 CE – 1882 CE (approx.) · capital Ghardaia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A self-governing league of Ibadi oasis towns in the Algerian Sahara, ruled by religious councils for nearly nine centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rustamid-imamate">Rustamid imamate</a> in 909, Ibadi refugees settled at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-ouargla">Ouargla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sedrata">Sedrata</a>, then moved into the barren Mzab valley of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. The first town, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-atteuf">El Atteuf</a>, was founded around 1012, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bou-noura">Bou Noura</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghardaia">Ghardaïa</a>, Melika and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beni-isguen">Beni Isguen</a>, the Pentapolis of the Mzab.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each walled hilltop town was governed by a council of clerics, the halqa of the azzaba, together with assemblies of lay notables, under strict Ibadi religious law. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozabites">Mozabites</a> dug deep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and ingenious dams to water their palm groves, and their merchants formed trading communities in the cities of the north. Their compact architecture later inspired modern architects such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-corbusier">Le Corbusier</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 16th century the Mzab paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers</a>. It accepted a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1853 and was annexed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria</a> in 1882, keeping its religious institutions. The valley is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage</a> site.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="theocracy, 1012 CE – 1882 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">theocracy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:79.84%;width:17.31%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1012 CE – 1882 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ghardaia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ibadi Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mozabite Berber, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mzab, Pentapolis of the Mzab, Ibadi Mzab</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1447&amp;focus=mzab-confederation" aria-label="Mzab confederation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4774 997 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mzab confederation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5094 1253L5033 1155M5094 1253L5077 1108M5094 1253L5077 1108"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5033 1155h0M5077 1108h0M5077 1108h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5094 1253h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5094 1253h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ghardaia. <a href="/atlas?year=1447&amp;focus=mzab-confederation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
